{"prompt": "Write a 300+ word summary of the wikipedia page \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_III,_Count_of_Tripoli\". Do not use any commas and highlight at least 3 sections that has titles in markdown format, for example *highlighted section part 1*, *highlighted section part 2*, *highlighted section part 3*.", "response": "Raymond III was a prominent figure in the history of the Crusader states who served as the Count of Tripoli from 1177 until his death in 1191. He was the son of Raymond II and his mother was the Countess of Toulouse. His reign was marked by significant military campaigns and diplomatic efforts to secure the independence and stability of the County of Tripoli.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\nRaymond III was born in 1142 and was educated in the court of his father. He succeeded his father in 1177 after a period of political instability. Upon taking the throne he faced the challenge of consolidating power and defending the county against neighboring Muslim states. He was known for his strategic mind and his ability to negotiate with both Christian and Muslim leaders.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Diplomacy*\nDuring his rule Raymond III launched several military expeditions to expand the territory of Tripoli and to weaken the power of the Ayyubid dynasty. He formed alliances with other Crusader states such as the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Edessa. His diplomatic skills were crucial in maintaining peace and trade routes in the region. He also engaged in negotiations with the Sultan of Egypt to secure favorable terms for the Crusaders.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\nRaymond III died in 1191 and was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. His legacy is remembered for his contributions to the defense of the Crusader states and his role in the political landscape of the Levant. He is often cited as a key figure in the history of the Third Crusade due to his interactions with Richard the Lionheart. His efforts helped to stabilize the region during a turbulent period in medieval history."}
{"prompt": "Generate a summary of the following passage in all capital letters:\n\nWorld War II or the Second World War, often abbreviated as WWII or WW2, was a global conflict that lasted from 1939 to 1945. The vast majority of the world's countries, including all of the great powers, fought as part of two opposing military alliances: the Allies and the Axis. Many participants threw their economic, industrial, and scientific capabilities behind this total war, blurring the distinction between civilian and military resources. Aircraft played a major role, enabling the strategic bombing of population centres and the delivery of the only two nuclear weapons ever used in war. World War II was by far the deadliest conflict in history, resulting in an estimated 70 to 85 million fatalities, mostly among civilians. Tens of millions died due to genocides (including the Holocaust), starvation, massacres, and disease. In the wake of Axis defeat, Germany, Austria and Japan were occupied, and war crimes tribunals were conducted against German and Japanese leaders.\n\n", "response": "WORLD WAR II, OR THE SECOND WORLD WAR, OFTEN ABBREVIATED AS WWII OR WW2, WAS A GLOBAL CONFLICT THAT LASTED FROM 1939 TO 1945. THE VAST MAJORITY OF THE WORLD'S COUNTRIES, INCLUDING ALL OF THE GREAT POWERS, FIGHT AS PART OF TWO OPPOSING MILITARY ALLIANCES: THE ALLIES AND THE AXIS. MANY PARTICIPANTS THREW THEIR ECONOMIC, INDUSTRIAL, AND SCIENTIFIC CAPABILITIES BEHIND THIS TOTAL WAR, BLURRING THE DISTINCTION BETWEEN CIVILIAN AND MILITARY RESOURCES. AIRCRAFT PLAYED A MAJOR ROLE, ENABLING THE STRATEGIC BOMBING OF POPULATION CENTRES AND THE DELIVERY OF THE ONLY TWO NUCLEAR WEAPONS EVER USED IN WAR. WORLD WAR II WAS BY FAR THE DEADLIEST CONFLICT IN HISTORY, RESULTING IN AN ESTIMATED 70 TO 85 MILLION FATALITIES, MOSTLY AMONG CIVILIANS. TENS OF MILLIONS DIED DUE TO GENOCIDES (INCLUDING THE HOLOCAUST), STARVATION, MASSACRES, AND DISEASE. IN THE WAKE OF AXIS DEFEAT, GERMANY, AUSTRIA AND JAPAN WERE OCCUPIED, AND WAR CRIMES TRIBUNALS WERE CONDUCTED AGAINST GERMAN AND JAPANESE LEADERS."}
